    />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">



<div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Two measurement bases, split by the nature of the holding</div><div class="principle">ASBE No. 2 governs an investing enterprise's LONG-TERM EQUITY INVESTMENTS in SUBSIDIARIES, ASSOCIATES, and JOINT VENTURES – applying EITHER the COST METHOD (for investments in subsidiaries, WITHIN the investor's own SEPARATE, non-consolidated financial statements) or the EQUITY METHOD (for investments in ASSOCIATES and joint ventures) – broadly PARALLELING the SAME dichotomy found under IAS 27 and IAS 28 internationally, though with a GENUINELY distinctive treatment for combinations UNDER COMMON CONTROL (Section 6).</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Investments in subsidiaries, associates, and joint ventures</div><div class="principle">This Standard applies to LONG-TERM EQUITY INVESTMENTS – those held with the INTENT of exercising CONTROL, joint control, or significant INFLUENCE over the investee, RATHER than short-term trading purposes (which would instead fall WITHIN the financial instruments standards, ASBE No. 22).</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>The Cost Method – Investments in Subsidiaries (Separate Statements)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Recording the investment at cost, recognising dividends as income</div><div class="text-supporting">In the investor's OWN separate (non-consolidated) financial statements, an investment in a SUBSIDIARY is recorded at COST, with dividends DECLARED by the subsidiary recognised as INVESTMENT INCOME (EXCEPT to the extent a dividend represents a RETURN of the ORIGINAL investment cost, rather than a distribution of POST-acquisition profits). The SUBSIDIARY's OWN results are NOT otherwise reflected in the investor's separate statements – full CONSOLIDATION occurs SEPARATELY, under ASBE No. 33.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>The Equity Method – Associates and Joint Ventures</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Adjusting the investment for the investor's share of the investee's results</div><div class="text-supporting">For investments in ASSOCIATES and JOINT VENTURES, the EQUITY METHOD applies – the INVESTMENT is initially recorded at COST, then ADJUSTED each period to reflect the investor's SHARE of the investee's PROFIT or LOSS, OTHER comprehensive income, and OTHER changes in the investee's NET assets – INCREASING or DECREASING the investment's carrying amount ACCORDINGLY, WITHOUT the investor separately RECOGNISING the investee's individual assets and liabilities.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Determining Significant Influence and Joint Control</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">The thresholds determining which measurement basis applies</div><div class="text-supporting">SIGNIFICANT INFLUENCE – the power to PARTICIPATE in the investee's FINANCIAL and operating POLICY decisions, WITHOUT full control – is generally PRESUMED where the investor holds BETWEEN 20% and 50% of the investee's VOTING rights (though OTHER qualitative indicators, such as BOARD representation, can also establish significant influence at DIFFERENT ownership levels). JOINT CONTROL exists where DECISIONS about relevant activities REQUIRE the UNANIMOUS consent of the parties SHARING control.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>THE KEY DIVERGENCE – Business Combinations Under Common Control</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">A predecessor-cost method, not the acquisition method</div><div class="text-supporting">WHERE a business combination occurs BETWEEN entities that are ULTIMATELY controlled by the SAME party or parties BOTH BEFORE and AFTER the combination (a "COMMON CONTROL" combination), ASBE requires the ACQUIRING entity to measure the LONG-TERM equity investment (or the combined net ASSETS) at the CARRYING AMOUNT reflected in the COMBINED entity's CONSOLIDATED financial statements (a "PREDECESSOR COST" or POOLING-OF-INTERESTS-STYLE approach) – RATHER than at FAIR VALUE under the ACQUISITION method IFRS 3 REQUIRES for essentially ALL business combinations, REGARDLESS of common control. This is ONE of the MOST significant, GENUINELY Chinese-specific departures from INTERNATIONAL standards WITHIN the entire ASBE suite.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Why Common-Control Combinations Are So Common in China</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">A reflection of China's state-owned enterprise restructuring landscape</div><div class="text-supporting">COMMON-CONTROL combinations are GENUINELY frequent in China, LARGELY due to the PREVALENCE of STATE-OWNED enterprise groups RESTRUCTURING their subsidiaries and business UNITS – transfers and combinations WITHIN a single ultimate state-controlled GROUP structure. Requiring FAIR VALUE remeasurement (and the associated GOODWILL recognition) for EVERY such internal restructuring would ARGUABLY overstate the ECONOMIC substance of what is, IN PRACTICE, an INTERNAL reorganisation RATHER than an ARM'S-LENGTH acquisition – the POLICY rationale UNDERLYING this DISTINCTIVE treatment.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Worked Example – Applying the Equity Method</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Adjusting the investment for the investor's share of profit</div>
<div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A 30% investment in an associate</div><div class="wk-body">A company acquires a 30% interest in an associate for RMB 60,000,000. During the year, the associate reports a net profit of RMB 20,000,000 and pays no dividends.</div><div class="wk-answer">The investor recognises its SHARE of the associate's profit: RMB 20,000,000 × 30% = <strong>RMB 6,000,000</strong>, INCREASING the carrying amount of the investment to RMB 60,000,000 + RMB 6,000,000 = <strong>RMB 66,000,000</strong>, with the RMB 6,000,000 also recognised as INVESTMENT INCOME in the investor's Income Statement.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Worked Example – Initial Measurement Under Common Control</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Carrying amount, not fair value</div>
<div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A parent company acquiring a sister subsidiary</div><div class="wk-body">A parent company, as part of an internal group restructuring, acquires 100% of a sister subsidiary (also ultimately controlled by the SAME parent both before and after the transaction). The subsidiary's net assets have a CARRYING amount of RMB 90,000,000 in the group's consolidated accounts, though an independent valuer estimates their FAIR VALUE at RMB 130,000,000.</div><div class="wk-answer">Because this is a COMMON CONTROL combination, the acquiring entity records the investment at the subsidiary's CARRYING amount of <strong>RMB 90,000,000</strong> – NOT the RMB 130,000,000 fair value – and NO goodwill is recognised, CONTRASTING sharply with how the SAME transaction would be accounted for under IFRS 3's acquisition method.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">What ASBE No. 2 requires</div><div class="check-list"><span>Classification and carrying amounts of long-term equity investments</span><span>Basis for determining significant influence or joint control</span><span>Whether a business combination occurred under common control, and the measurement basis applied</span></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
<div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Whether a business combination is genuinely "under common control," given the timing and substance of ultimate ownership</span><span>Whether significant influence exists despite an ownership stake outside the 20–50% presumptive range</span></div></div>
<div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Fair value of an equity-method investee's identifiable net assets, where relevant to impairment assessment</span></div></div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Relationship to IAS 27/28 and IFRS 3</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Comparison – ASBE 2 vs IAS 27/28 and IFRS 3</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">ASBE 2</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IAS 27/28 / IFRS 3</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Cost/equity method dichotomy</span><span>Same structure</span><span>Same structure – converged</span></div>
<div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Common-control combinations</span><span>Predecessor cost (carrying amount), no goodwill</span><span>NO special exemption – full acquisition method applies regardless of common control</span></div>
</div>
<div class="note">The COST/equity method FRAMEWORK is thoroughly converged – the common-control TREATMENT is a genuinely SIGNIFICANT, WELL-KNOWN structural divergence, reflecting China's DISTINCTIVE state-enterprise ownership landscape.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where every long-term equity investment event lands</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Investment carrying amount</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span></span><span>At cost, or adjusted under the equity method</span></div>
<div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Share of investee profit/loss</span><span><span class="badge bp">IS</span></span><span>Recognised as investment income under the equity method</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Related Standards</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Standard</span><span>Connection</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASBE No. 20</span><span>Business Combinations – supplies the general acquisition-method framework this Standard's common-control exception departs from</span></div>
<div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASBE No. 33</span><span>Consolidated Financial Statements – governs full consolidation of subsidiaries, complementing this Standard's separate-statement cost method</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s15"><span class="num">15</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cn span2">
<div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Business combination under common control</div><div class="glossary-def">A combination in which all combining entities are ultimately controlled by the same party or parties, both before and after the combination, measured at predecessor carrying amounts rather than fair value.</div></div>
<div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Significant influence</div><div class="glossary-def">The power to participate in an investee's financial and operating policy decisions, generally presumed at a 20–50% voting interest, without amounting to control.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="legend"><div class="ltitle">Financial statement key (Chinese GAAP / ASBE terminology)</div><div class="lrow"><div class="litem"><span class="badge bs">BS</span> Balance Sheet (资产负债表) </div><div class="litem"><span class="badge bp">IS</span> Income Statement (利润表) </div><div class="litem"><span class="badge be">SCOE</span> Statement of Changes in Owners' Equity (所有者权益变动表) </div><div class="litem"><span class="badge bc">CFS</span> Cash Flow Statement (现金流量表)</div></div></div>


</div>
